-- title:
--   Connected–étale splitting of a finite Hopf algebra over mathbf Fₚ
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a commutative ring which is a Hopf algebra over $\mathbf F_p = \mathbb{Z}/p$, cocommutative, and finite as an $\mathbf F_p$-module. The assertion is the existence of two further commutative rings $B_c$ and $B_e$, each carrying the structure of a cocommutative Hopf algebra over $\mathbf F_p$ and finite as an $\mathbf F_p$-module, together with bialgebra maps $q_c\colon B \to B_c$, $\pi_e\colon B \to B_e$, $\sigma\colon B_e \to B$ and $\Theta\colon B \to B_c \otimes_{\mathbf F_p} B_e$, subject to the following: $B_c$ is a local ring; $B_e$ is reduced and formally unramified over $\mathbf F_p$; $q_c$ and $\pi_e$ are surjective; the kernel of the underlying algebra map of $\pi_e$ is the nilradical of $B$; $\pi_e \circ \sigma$ is the identity bialgebra map of $B_e$, and $\sigma$ is the unique $\mathbf F_p$-algebra homomorphism $\tau\colon B_e \to B$ with $\pi_e \circ \tau = \mathrm{id}$; the kernel of $q_c$ is the ideal of $B$ generated by the image under $\sigma$ of the kernel of the counit $B_e \to \mathbf F_p$; and $\Theta$ is bijective and is given on every $b \in B$ by applying $q_c \otimes \pi_e$ to the comultiplication $\Delta(b)$.
--
--   This is the connected–étale splitting $G \cong G^{0} \times G_{\mathrm{red}}$ of a finite commutative group scheme over the perfect field $\mathbf F_p$, written on the Hopf-algebra side: $B_c$ is the coordinate ring of the connected component of the identity, $B_e$ that of the reduced (hence étale) quotient, and $\sigma$ the canonical splitting. It is invoked in the analysis of Cartier duality for such Hopf algebras and, at the level of towers, in the splitting of a $p$-divisible group over $\mathbf F_p$ into its connected and étale parts.
